  "targetBmiTitle": "Body mass index",
  "targetBmiWhat": "Your weight divided by your height squared. It puts one number on the relationship between the two.",
  "targetBmiWhy": "Every other target on this screen is tuned by which band you fall into, so it is the starting point rather than a score in itself.",
  "targetBmiBands": "Under 18.5 is under range · 18.5 to 25 is healthy · 25 to 30 is above · 30 and over is well above.",
  "targetBmiCaveat": "BMI cannot tell muscle from fat, so athletes often read high while being perfectly healthy. Treat it as a rough guide, not a verdict.",
  "targetCaloriesTitle": "Daily calories",
  "targetCaloriesWhat": "An estimate of what your body burns in a lightly active day, adjusted toward your goal.",
  "targetCaloriesWhy": "Eating roughly this much keeps weight steady. The adjustment is deliberately small so progress is gradual rather than punishing.",
  "targetCaloriesHow": "Calculated with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation from your weight, height, and age, multiplied by 1.45 for daily movement. Because the app does not ask your sex, the result is averaged between the male and female formulas, which can be off by roughly 80 kcal either way.",
  "targetCaloriesAdjustUnder": "300 kcal was added to help you gain steadily.",
  "targetCaloriesAdjustHealthy": "No adjustment — this is your maintenance level.",
  "targetCaloriesAdjustAbove": "350 kcal was removed for gradual loss.",
  "targetCaloriesAdjustWellAbove": "500 kcal was removed for steady, sustainable loss.",
  "targetWaterTitle": "Water",
  "targetWaterWhat": "Roughly 33 ml for every kilogram you weigh, which is why a heavier body gets a larger figure.",
  "targetWaterWhy": "Being even mildly short on fluid shows up as tiredness and headaches long before you feel thirsty, and it skews several blood markers.",
  "targetWaterCaveat": "Drink more in heat, during exercise, or when unwell. Food contributes too, so this is not all from a glass.",
  "targetStepsTitle": "Steps",
  "targetStepsWhat": "A daily movement target, starting from 8,000 and shifted for your situation.",
  "targetStepsWhy": "Regular walking does more for blood pressure, blood sugar, and mood than almost any other single habit, and it needs no equipment.",
  "targetStepsHow": "Raised for an above-range BMI, lowered again when well above so joints are not overloaded at the start, reduced past 60, and raised for under-30s.",
